--TEST--
`Core\Html::sanitize` grants the three global attributes that describe the text and none of the three a page selects on, so `class`, `id` and `style` reach no rebuilt document — `rule:core-classes/html-sanitize`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The allowlist grants attributes the same way it grants elements, and what it
// grants globally is three that each say something about the text: what it is
// called, what language it is in, which way it reads. None of the three is a
// selector, and that boundary is what this case asks about.
tainted string $submitted =
    "<p title=\"a note\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"" .
    " class=\"modal-overlay\" id=\"submit\" style=\"position:fixed\">Hello.</p>";

Core\Html\Markup $clean = Core\Html::sanitize($submitted);
string $source = Core\Html::toSource($clean, "asking which attributes the rebuild granted");
echo $source, "\n";

// Counted rather than read off that line, because a rebuild that grew a value
// test for one of the three refused would still print plausibly on whatever
// input it was tried with. `class` and `id` are the two a receiving page's own
// stylesheet and script select on, and the meaning of either value lives in a
// document this member never sees, so there is no value test to write here;
// `style` is a second grammar with no parser here at all.
array<string> $granted = ["title", "lang", "dir"];
array<string> $refused = ["class", "id", "style"];
int $kept = 0;
int $leaked = 0;
foreach ($granted as string $name) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($source, $name)) {
        $kept = $kept + 1;
    }
}
foreach ($refused as string $name) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($source, $name)) {
        $leaked = $leaked + 1;
    }
}
echo "granted ", $kept, " of 3, refused ", $leaked, " of 3\n";
--EXPECT--
<p title="a note" lang="en" dir="ltr">Hello.</p>
granted 3 of 3, refused 0 of 3
